[WORK]
UID: WORK-ANNOTATION-NODES
TITLE: An annotation class: question, contradiction, comment, gap, known issue
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
An annotation class attachable to ANY node, chainable for threads. The
operator names five kinds as A SUBSET of what might be possible based on
semantic rules yet to be come up with -- the class is open-ended, not this
enumeration: question (needs clarity), contradiction (conflicting nodes, not
main), comment (anything, like a preference on the impl), gap
(non-exhaustive coverage, not main), known issue (accepted gap). These need
not be separate node types -- a field suffices unless a specific shape earns
its keep as a forcing function; the operator's stated preference (not a
ruling): split when the semantics are very different. This supersedes the
earlier sidecar-for-edge-fields design.

Lifecycle, not settled: leaning NOT persisted to main -- work happens on a
long-lived branch and completed plans go to main, because some plans cannot
be specced upfront (needs spike or research) -- but the operator wants clean
history and has not committed. One rule they did state: work cannot start
while conflicts are open.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Verbatim on the open-ended class: "In the past I mentioned a class of nodes
that may not persist to main. Below covers a subset of what might be
possible based on semantic rules we come up with."

Ambiguity the operator flagged themselves -- USE_CASE's home: "coverage: use
case? I think it slots here as a form of contract but could also be seen as
work (test coverage)."

Verbatim on the lifecycle: "not 100% settled on not main. I'd rather have a
clean history, but some plans cannot be specced upfront, e.g., needs spike
or research. I'm still leaning not main, use a long-lived branch, completed
plans go to main. Work cannot start if conflicts though."

Overlap to resolve at grooming: the grammar ALREADY carries an empty
COMMENTARY element (STANDING open/closed/withdrawn, CLOSES_ON, EDGE;
DEC-COMMENTARY-IS-THE-FIFTH-FAMILY is open, nothing is one yet). The dump
does not mention it -- whether this annotation class extends, supersedes, or
coexists with COMMENTARY is unasked and unanswered.
<<<
